.SH NAME
ktm-guide-control-flow \- Control flow (chapter 5 of the ktm language guide)
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
\fBif\fR and \fBelse\fR, the three loops, the range-based \fBfor\fR, \fBbreak\fR and \fBcontinue\fR, and \fBgoto\fR\&. switch (see ktm-guide-switch(7)) has its own chapter.
.SH Braces are required
.PP
The body of \fBif\fR, \fBelse\fR, \fBfor\fR, \fBwhile\fR and \fBdo\fR is always a block in braces, even when it is one statement. \fBelse if\fR is the one exception. This rules out a whole class of bugs where a second line looks guarded but isn't.
.PP
This does not compile:
.RS 4
.EX
void main() {
    i64 x = 1;
    if (x) x = 2;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
braces required: the body of `if` goes in `{ ... }`
.EE
.RE
.SH Parentheses are optional
.PP
The condition can be written in parentheses, as in C, or without them, as in Go. Both mean the same, and one program can mix them. Without parentheses, the condition runs up to the \fB{\fR\&.
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
void main() {
    i64 a = 1, b = 2;
    if (a < b) {
        printf("with parentheses\en");
    }
    if a < b {
        printf("without them\en");
    }
    if (a + b) * 2 > 5 {
        printf("a condition can start with a parenthesis of its own\en");
    }
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
with parentheses
without them
a condition can start with a parenthesis of its own
.EE
.RE
.PP
The rest of this chapter uses parentheses; every example works without them too.
.SH if and else
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
void classify(i64 n) {
    if (n < 0) {
        printf("%d is negative\en", n);
    } else if (n == 0) {
        printf("%d is zero\en", n);
    } else if (n < 10) {
        printf("%d is small\en", n);
    } else {
        printf("%d is large\en", n);
    }
}

void main() {
    classify(\-5);
    classify(0);
    classify(7);
    classify(100);
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
\-5 is negative
0 is zero
7 is small
100 is large
.EE
.RE
.PP
The condition is any number or pointer: zero and \fBnull\fR are false, everything else true.
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
void main() {
    u8 *name = "ktm";
    i64 count = 0;
    if (name) {
        printf("has a name\en");
    }
    if (!count) {
        printf("count is zero\en");
    }
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
has a name
count is zero
.EE
.RE
.SS A declaration in the if
.PP
A declaration can come before the condition, separated by \fB;\fR\&. It is visible in the \fBif\fR and in its \fBelse\fR branches, and nowhere else.
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
i64 lookup(u8 *key) { return key[0] == 'a' ? 42 : \-1; }

void main() {
    if (i64 v = lookup("apple"); v >= 0) {
        printf("found %d\en", v);
    } else {
        printf("missing\en");
    }
    if (i64 v = lookup("kiwi"); v >= 0) {
        printf("found %d\en", v);
    } else {
        printf("missing (v is %d)\en", v);
    }
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
found 42
missing (v is \-1)
.EE
.RE
.PP
A declaration alone is a condition too: the variable's value is tested.
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
u8 *findUser(i64 id) { return id == 1 ? "ada" : null; }

void main() {
    if (u8 *name = findUser(1)) {
        printf("user 1 is %s\en", name);
    }
    if (u8 *name = findUser(2)) {
        printf("user 2 is %s\en", name);
    } else {
        printf("no user 2\en");
    }
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
user 1 is ada
no user 2
.EE
.RE
.SH while
.PP
\fBwhile\fR runs its body as long as the condition is true. The condition is tested before each pass, so the body may not run at all.
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
void main() {
    i64 n = 27, steps = 0;
    while (n != 1) {
        n = n % 2 == 0 ? n / 2 : 3 * n + 1;
        steps++;
    }
    printf("27 reaches 1 in %d steps\en", steps);

    i64 never = 0;
    while (never > 0) {
        printf("not printed\en");
    }
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
27 reaches 1 in 111 steps
.EE
.RE
.SH do ... while
.PP
\fBdo\fR tests the condition after each pass, so the body runs at least once. The \fBwhile\fR at the end takes a \fB;\fR\&.
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
void main() {
    i64 n = 0;
    do {
        printf("pass %d\en", n);
        n++;
    } while (n < 3);

    i64 big = 100;
    do {
        printf("runs once, even though %d >= 3\en", big);
    } while (big < 3);
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
pass 0
pass 1
pass 2
runs once, even though 100 >= 3
.EE
.RE
.SH for
.PP
The classic three-part \fBfor\fR: a start, a condition tested before each pass, and a step run after each pass. A variable declared in the start belongs to the loop.
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
void main() {
    for (i64 i = 0; i < 5; i++) {
        printf("%d ", i);
    }
    printf("\en");
    for (i64 i = 10; i > 0; i \-= 3) {
        printf("%d ", i);
    }
    printf("\en");
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
0 1 2 3 4 
10 7 4 1 
.EE
.RE
.PP
Each part can be left out. Several variables and several steps are separated by commas.
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
void main() {
    for (i64 lo = 0, hi = 9; lo < hi; lo++, hi\-\-) {
        printf("(%d,%d) ", lo, hi);
    }
    printf("\en");

    i64 k = 0;
    for (; k < 3;) {
        k++;
    }
    printf("k = %d\en", k);
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
(0,9) (1,8) (2,7) (3,6) (4,5) 
k = 3
.EE
.RE
.PP
Without parentheses, \fBfor\fR also has Go's two short forms: \fBfor cond { }\fR is a \fBwhile\fR, and \fBfor { }\fR loops until something leaves it.
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
void main() {
    i64 n = 0;
    for n < 3 {
        n++;
    }
    for {
        n++;
        if n == 10 { break; }
    }
    printf("%d\en", n);
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
10
.EE
.RE
.SH break and continue
.PP
\fBbreak\fR leaves the innermost loop; \fBcontinue\fR skips to its next pass (in a \fBfor\fR, the step still runs).
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
void main() {
    for (i64 i = 0; i < 10; i++) {
        if (i == 2) { continue; }
        if (i == 6) { break; }
        printf("%d ", i);
    }
    printf("\en");
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
0 1 3 4 5 
.EE
.RE
.PP
In nested loops, they act on the innermost one:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
void main() {
    for (i64 row = 0; row < 3; row++) {
        for (i64 col = 0; col < 3; col++) {
            if (col > row) { break; }
            printf("(%d,%d)", row, col);
        }
    }
    printf("\en");
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
(0,0)(1,0)(1,1)(2,0)(2,1)(2,2)
.EE
.RE
.PP
To leave several loops at once, return from a function, or use \fBgoto\fR (below).
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
bool findPair(i64 *nums, i64 n, i64 target, i64 *a, i64 *b) {
    for (i64 i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        for (i64 j = i + 1; j < n; j++) {
            if (nums[i] + nums[j] == target) {
                *a = i;
                *b = j;
                return true;
            }
        }
    }
    return false;
}

void main() {
    i64 nums[] = {3, 8, 11, 4};
    i64 a, b;
    if (findPair(nums, 4, 12, &a, &b)) {
        printf("nums[%d] + nums[%d] = 12\en", a, b);
    }
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
nums[1] + nums[3] = 12
.EE
.RE
.SH The range-based for
.PP
\fBfor (T x : items)\fR runs once per element, in order. It works on:
.IP \(bu 2
fixed-size arrays,
.IP \(bu 2
slices (\fBT[]\fR), \fBString\fRs included,
.IP \(bu 2
any struct with \fBdata\fR and \fBlen\fR fields (or \fBentries\fR and \fBsize\fR), such as vec.Vec (see ktm-vec(3)),
.IP \(bu 2
a map.Map (see ktm-map(3)): each entry, in the order the keys were added.
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
import vec;

void main() {
    i64 primes[] = {2, 3, 5, 7, 11};
    i64 sum = 0;
    for (auto p : primes) {
        sum += p;
    }
    printf("sum = %d\en", sum);

    u8[] word = "ktm";
    for (u8 c : word) {
        printf("[%c]", c);
    }
    printf("\en");

    vec.Vec<f64> v = vec.new<f64>();
    v.push(1.5);
    v.push(2.5);
    for (f64 x : v) {
        printf("%.1f ", x);
    }
    printf("\en");
    v.free();
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
sum = 28
[k][t][m]
1.5 2.5 
.EE
.RE
.PP
Over a map, the loop variable is an entry, with the key in \fB\&.key\fR and the value in \fB\&.value\fR\&. Deleted keys are skipped:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
import map;

void main() {
    map.Map<u8[], i64> stock = map.new<u8[], i64>();
    stock.set("apples", 12);
    stock.set("pears", 0);
    stock.set("plums", 7);
    stock.delete("pears");
    for (auto e : stock) {
        printf("%.*s: %d\en", e.key.len, e.key.ptr, e.value);
    }
    stock.free();
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
apples: 12
plums: 7
.EE
.RE
.PP
The loop variable is a copy of the element. Changing it changes nothing in the array; to change the elements, loop over the index:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
void main() {
    i64 nums[] = {1, 2, 3};
    for (auto n : nums) {
        n *= 10;
    }
    printf("%d %d %d\en", nums[0], nums[1], nums[2]);
    for (i64 i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
        nums[i] *= 10;
    }
    printf("%d %d %d\en", nums[0], nums[1], nums[2]);
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
1 2 3
10 20 30
.EE
.RE
.PP
\fBbreak\fR and \fBcontinue\fR work in a range-based \fBfor\fR as in any loop:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
void main() {
    u8[] text = "find the q here";
    i64 at = 0;
    for (u8 c : text) {
        if (c == 'q') { break; }
        at++;
    }
    printf("q is at %d\en", at);
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
q is at 9
.EE
.RE
.SH goto
.PP
\fBgoto label;\fR jumps to \fBlabel:\fR in the same function. It is useful for retry loops and for leaving nested loops at once.
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
void main() {
    i64 tries = 0;
retry:
    tries++;
    if (tries < 3) {
        printf("try %d failed\en", tries);
        goto retry;
    }
    printf("succeeded on try %d\en", tries);
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
try 1 failed
try 2 failed
succeeded on try 3
.EE
.RE
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
void main() {
    for (i64 i = 0; i < 10; i++) {
        for (i64 j = 0; j < 10; j++) {
            if (i * j == 42) {
                printf("%d * %d = 42\en", i, j);
                goto done;
            }
        }
    }
done:
    printf("done\en");
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
6 * 7 = 42
done
.EE
.RE
.PP
A \fBgoto\fR can't jump forward past a \fBdefer\fR into the rest of its block, since the deferred statement would then run without its setup having happened.
